lesson writing-where-each-letter-starts v=1 format=teach grade=1 unit=1 subject=writing levels=[easy, medium, hard] {
  title "Where each letter starts"
  goal "Start a letter at the right place and move the pencil the right way."

  look {
    scene 33x14 {
      row all at=canvas(1, 1) space=1 align=end {
        formletter big letter="c" copies=2 write=1 label=true
        art pic asset="cat"
      }
    }
    say """
      Cat starts with c. The pink dot is where the pencil starts. The arrows show which way it
      goes. A c starts near the top and curls round to the left, like the start of a circle.
      Letters that move the same way are a family: c is a curly letter, and so are a, d, o and s.
      """
  }
  do {
    level easy {
      worked writing.letter-family ch="d" level=medium
    }
    show writing.letter-family ch="h" easy-ch="c"
    show writing.letter-family ch="t" hard-ch="d"
    show writing.same-family k=4 q=1 easy-k=0
    show writing.same-family k=3 q=2
    show writing.trace-and-write ch="k"
    show writing.family-odd-out s=0 q=2 easy-s=1
    show writing.family-odd-out s=3 q=1 hard-s=4
    practice writing.first-stroke count=1 seed=4
  }
  try stars=2 {
    show writing.family-claim c=1 easy-c=4 hard-c=0
  }
  try stars=3 {
    show writing.family-word k=0
  }
  remember {
    say "Start at the dot. Go the way the arrow goes."
  }

  grown-ups """
    The family questions are marked by the machine; the tracing is yours to look at. Watch the
    start rather than the finished letter. A letter started at the bottom can look fine at this
    size and still be slow and untidy later, when it has to be joined and written quickly. Short
    practice often is better than a page at once: five or six of one letter, then something else.
    The odd-one-out questions are built so the odd letter looks like the others: d is tall like h
    and b but starts with a curl. The two-star question is Tia's claim about a letter, and the point
    is that a family is about how a letter starts, not how it looks. The three-star question has no
    word to copy: let the child try words out loud and check each letter before writing.
    """
  level easy {
    grown-ups "At this level d's curly start is shown once in the teacher's pen before c and t, the letters that start alike are h and n, the odd ones out are n among the curly letters and y among the zigzag letters, the letter to trace and the first stroke are as written, Tia is right that b is a bridge letter, and the three-star is as written."
  }
  level hard {
    grown-ups "At this level the families to name are h and d, which starts like c against its look, the letters that start alike are as written, the odd ones out are d among h, b and k, and p among the curly letters, the first stroke is the round ones and the zigzags, Tia calls d a ladder letter because it is tall, and the three-star is as written."
  }
}
